2014-10-30 15:51 GMT+03:00 Pierre Abbat <phma@bezitopo.org>:

> On Thursday, October 30, 2014 13:41:29 Gleki Arxokuna wrote:
> > lo jbopre pu jmaji lo sofybakni purdi lo vrici gugde
> > Lojbanists gathered a garden arriving from various countries.
> >
> > I think this example is broken since {lo vrici gugde} or {lo gugde poi
> > vrici} can describe even one country various in vrici2.
> >
> > I suppose that somehow we need to show that there were different from
> each
> > other.
> > Is it just {noi simxu lo ka ce'u ce'u nalsimsa} or can we use {ni'e
> vrici}
> > here?
>
> I'd say {lo ficysi'u gugde} or {loi ficysi'u gugde}.
>

> > If {ni'e} then how does it work under official grammar?
> >
> > {lo jbopre pu jmaji lo ni'e vrici boi gugde} doesn't parse officially.
> Then
> > what?
>
> {boi} terminates a string of letters or numbers. I don't see a terminator
> for
> {ni'e}. Try {vau}. If that doesn't work, we need a terminator for {ni'e}.
>

It is to be expanded by the parser to
([lo jbopre KU] [CU {pu jmaji} {<(=C2=B9ni'e vrici TEhU=C2=B9) BOI> <lo gug=
de KU>}
VAU])

However, nothing works. The question is. If this parse is okay can I use it
even before approving by LLG? Can it be accepted as a known bug?
Or is it an intended limitation of the grammar?

Finally, is it smudra?
If standard PA count over the number of countries Lojbanists arrive from
can {ni'e vrici te'u} count over versatility of those countries?



> Pierre
> --
> The gostak pelled at the fostin lutt for darfs for her martle plave.
> The darfs had smibbed, the lutt was thale, and the pilter had nothing
> snave.
